<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastern Front (World War II)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Great Patriotic War, German–Soviet War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eastern Front, also known as the Great Patriotic War or the German–Soviet War, was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> fought between the European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, from 22 June 1941 to 9 May 1945. It began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a>, the Axis invasion of the Soviet Union, which came close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Soviet victories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-bagration">Operation Bagration</a> turned the war, which ended with the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> waged a war of annihilation, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a>, starvation and massacres; around 30 million of the war's deaths occurred on this front.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a>, the Bolshevik government signed the separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a> in March 1918 and ceded control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and other areas to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>. In Mein Kampf (1925) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> argued for Lebensraum, new territory for Germans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, where most of the existing inhabitants were to be exterminated or deported and the remainder used as slave labour. Throughout the 1930s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> industrialised through five-year plans, turning towards military production in the mid-1930s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a> began before dawn on 22 June 1941, when 99 of 190 German divisions, with Romanian, Italian, Slovak and Hungarian forces, struck from the Baltic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. Soviet intelligence had given warning that was not acted on. Operation Typhoon, begun on 30 September, encircled Soviet forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyazma">Vyazma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryansk">Bryansk</a>, but autumn rain turned the roads to mud, and Army Group North began the 900-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-leningrad">Siege of Leningrad</a>. The Soviet counter-offensive of December removed the immediate threat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><p>In 1942 the offensive known as Fall Blau, launched on 28 June, drove towards the Don, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. On 19 November Operation Uranus broke through Romanian lines, and two Soviet fronts met at Kalach on 23 November, trapping 300,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a>. After the German failure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-bagration">Operation Bagration</a>, launched on 22 June 1944, smashed Army Group Centre. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>–Oder Offensive followed the Soviet entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 17 January 1945. The capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> followed, and on 8 August 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Eastern Front was the largest and deadliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the war: around 30 million of the estimated 70 to 85 million deaths occurred there. Soviet military deaths are estimated at 8.7 to 10 million and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> military deaths at 5 million, and the German armed forces suffered 80 per cent of their military deaths on this front. Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> waged a war of annihilation marked by scorched earth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a>, starvation and massacres, including plans known as Generalplan Ost and the Hunger Plan. The Soviet victory owed much to industry: thousands of trains carried factories from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> to areas east of the Urals in 1941.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eastern Front (World War II)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
